\section{Granularity Tradeoff}
\label{sec:granularity}

Quantization budget can in principle be allocated at three granularities, each with a distinct cost--benefit profile.
\emph{Layer-level} allocation (KVmix~\cite{li2025kvmix}, KVTuner~\cite{li2025kvtuner}) keeps the search space small ($L$ layers) but forces all heads in a layer to share one bitwidth.
This is fundamentally limited when within-layer variance is large: in Qwen2.5-7B Layer-0, per-head 2-bit deltas are 6.74, 2.12, 1.31, and 1.24---a more than $5\times$ spread that any layer-level assignment must average over.
\emph{Per-head} allocation (ours) exposes this heterogeneity to the solver and yields a 29.4\% PPL improvement on Qwen, at the cost of $H$ profiling passes; the cardinality constraint developed in \S\ref{sec:cardinality} renders the choice of solver irrelevant, amortizing the search overhead.
\emph{Token-level} allocation (ZipCache~\cite{he2024zipcache}, KIVI~\cite{liu2024kivi}) is the finest grain available but introduces runtime overhead from online importance scoring.
Importantly, per-head and per-token granularities are orthogonal: a head's bitwidth (static, determined offline) and a token's importance (dynamic, determined online) compose without conflict, so the two can be combined.
Per-head therefore occupies the sweet spot for static allocation---fine enough to capture the head-level heterogeneity that drives MCKP benefit on spread architectures, yet free of any per-step runtime cost.

\section{Budget Sweep}
\label{sec:budget_sweep}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Effect of compression ratio on Qwen2.5-7B. Constrained MCKP provides meaningful gains at moderate-to-aggressive compression ($\geq$4$\times$). Standard-resolution profiling.}
\label{tab:budget_sweep}
\begin{tabular}{cccc}
\toprule
Compression & Uniform PPL & Constr.\ MCKP PPL & $\Delta$ PPL \\
\midrule
2$\times$ & 10.10 & 10.66 & $-$0.56 \\
4$\times$ & 15.45 & 11.23 & $+$4.22 \\
8$\times$ & 14{,}759 & 907 & $+$13{,}852 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:budget_sweep} shows how the benefit of constrained allocation varies with compression ratio on Qwen2.5-7B.
At 2$\times$ compression, uniform 8-bit quantization is near-lossless (10.10 PPL vs.\ 9.81 bf16); constrained MCKP is slightly worse (10.66) due to unnecessary 2-bit assignments.
At 4$\times$, constrained MCKP reduces PPL from 15.45 to 11.23, a 27.3\% improvement; hires profiling further reduces this to 10.91 (Appendix~\ref{sec:profiling_resolution}).
At 8$\times$, both methods degrade catastrophically, but constrained MCKP (907) is an order of magnitude better than uniform (14{,}759) because the constraint still protects Layer-0 heads.

\section{Cross-Dataset Stability}
\label{sec:cross_dataset}

To verify that the profiled allocation is not overfit to the calibration corpus, we profile on C4 and evaluate on WikiText-2, and vice versa, at $4\times$ compression.
On Qwen2.5-7B, C4-calibrated MCKP achieves 11.29 PPL on WikiText-2 (vs.\ 15.45 uniform, a 27\% reduction), only 0.06 PPL above the WikiText-2-calibrated result (11.23).
In the reverse direction, WikiText-2-calibrated MCKP achieves 20.56 PPL on the C4 held-out split (vs.\ 35.42 uniform, a 42\% reduction; C4 bf16 baseline on this split is 15.32).
All three models show ${>}96\%$ bit-assignment agreement across calibration sources: Qwen 96.4\% (108/112 heads), Llama 97.7\% (250/256), and Mistral 96.5\% (247/256).
Qwen's agreement is the most informative, as all 112 heads have variable assignments; on Llama, 253 of 256 heads are forced to 4-bit by the $k{=}2$ constraint, inflating agreement.
The cross-dataset results reinforce the heterogeneity-driven narrative: on Qwen, the MCKP benefit is preserved across calibration sources; on Llama and Mistral, the lack of benefit is equally stable---Llama's C4-calibrated MCKP is 0.18 PPL worse than uniform, and Mistral shows similar degradation ($-$0.03 to $-$0.05 PPL).

\section{Aggressive Compression}
\label{sec:aggressive}

At $4\times$ compression, Llama and Mistral show minimal benefit from non-uniform allocation.
At more aggressive ratios, the picture changes: at $5.3\times$ (average 3 bits), constrained MCKP achieves $45\times$ lower PPL than uniform on Llama (57.60 vs.\ 2643.77) and $44\times$ on Mistral (11.91 vs.\ 519.11), because the constraint protects sensitive heads from 2-bit assignment.
At $8\times$, the budget is too constrained for any allocation to preserve quality (both methods degrade catastrophically).
The intermediate regime ($5.3\times$) provides the largest relative benefit.

\section{Profiling Resolution and Stability}
\label{sec:profiling_resolution}

We compare standard-resolution profiling (16 sequences $\times$ 512 tokens) against high-resolution profiling (64 sequences $\times$ 2048 tokens) on Qwen2.5-7B at $4\times$ compression.

High-resolution profiling improves constrained MCKP from 11.23 to 10.91 PPL ($-$0.32 absolute).
Relative to the bf16 baseline (9.81), this reduces the degradation from 1.42 to 1.10, a 22.5\% reduction.
The improvement comes from finer-grained sensitivity estimates: standard profiling detects non-zero 2-bit distortion in 37 of 112 heads, while high-resolution profiling detects 100 of 112 (2.7$\times$ more).

The additional signal changes 8 of 112 head assignments.
High-resolution profiling protects shallow layers (L1--L3) at higher bit-widths while compressing mid-layers (L5--L11) more aggressively---a redistribution that standard profiling misses because its distortion estimates for these heads fall below the noise floor.

\paragraph{Multi-seed stability.}
The profiled allocation is stable across calibration subsets.
Under hires profiling on Qwen2.5-7B, constrained MCKP yields mean PPL $10.99 \pm 0.06$ across 3 random calibration seeds (individual runs: 11.02, 10.90, 11.05), with std/mean below 0.6\%.
The bit distribution is identical across all seeds ($\{$2-bit: 8, 4-bit: 100, 8-bit: 4$\}$), and 101 of 112 heads (90.2\%) receive the same assignment in all three runs.
This stability confirms that the per-head sensitivity ranking is robust to calibration sampling noise.

\paragraph{Numerical precision.}
Float32 profiling (64 $\times$ 2048 tokens) yields an identical constrained allocation and PPL within seed variance (10.89 vs.\ 10.91, $\Delta = 0.02 < \sigma = 0.06$), confirming that bfloat16 precision is sufficient for the profiling step.

\paragraph{Per-layer optimality.}
To validate the additive distortion assumption underlying the MCKP formulation, we compare constrained MCKP against a per-layer brute-force oracle that exhaustively enumerates all $4^4 = 256$ bit-width combinations within each 4-head group on Qwen2.5-7B (hires profiling).
Of the 8 layers admitting multiple feasible allocations, MCKP matches the oracle exactly in 5; the remaining 3 show gaps of 0.018 (Layer~1), 0.014 (Layer~11), and 0.005 (Layer~4) PPL.
All three gaps involve repositioning a 2-bit assignment among heads with near-identical sensitivity within the same layer bit budget.
The negligible total gap ($<0.04$ PPL) confirms that the per-head additive distortion model closely approximates the true layer-level interaction.

\section{KV Separation Ablation}
\label{sec:kv_separation}

We test whether treating K and V caches as independent items in the MCKP solver---allowing different bit-widths for K and V within the same head---improves over joint allocation where K and V share a bit-width.

Joint allocation achieves 10.91 PPL; separated K/V allocation achieves 11.22 ($+$0.31 worse).
The degradation stems from K-side over-compression: the separated solver doubles the maximum number of 2-bit K items to 16 (vs.\ 8 in the joint formulation), since K and V are counted independently against the cardinality constraint.
Meanwhile, V-side distortion is near-zero for most heads, so the solver assigns all V items to 4-bit, concentrating compression entirely on K.

Of the 112 heads, 21 show K$\neq$V asymmetry in the separated allocation, all with K receiving a lower bit-width than V.
This systematic asymmetry suggests that K caches are inherently more compressible than V caches, consistent with the observation that key vectors primarily encode positional information while value vectors carry content.

Joint allocation is sufficient under the current constraint formulation.
Separated K/V allocation with per-side cardinality constraints---limiting 2-bit assignments independently for K and V---is a direction for future work.

\section{Runtime Benchmark}
\label{sec:runtime}

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\caption{PyTorch-level latency on Qwen2.5-7B (single NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 (96\,GB), prompt\_len=256, gen\_len=128, mean of 10 runs). All conditions store KV in bf16 with simulated per-head quantize/dequantize calls; peak GPU memory is identical across rows.}
\label{tab:runtime}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Condition & Prefill (ms) & Decode (ms/tok) & Throughput (tok/s) \\
\midrule
Baseline bf16       & 45.86 & 33.78 & 29.6 \\
Uniform 4-bit       & 54.25 & 42.46 & 23.6 \\
Constrained MCKP    & 57.03 & 46.09 & 21.8 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:runtime} reports end-to-end latency for three KV cache configurations.
Uniform 4-bit quantization adds 25.7\% decode overhead relative to the bf16 baseline (42.46 vs.\ 33.78~ms/tok), reflecting per-head quantize/dequantize calls at every attention step.
Constrained MCKP adds a further 8.5\% over uniform (46.09 vs.\ 42.46~ms/tok), because mixed-precision allocation dispatches different dequantization kernels for 2-bit, 4-bit, and 8-bit heads within the same layer.
The MCKP solver itself runs in ${\sim}5$~ms and is invoked once at initialization, contributing negligibly to end-to-end latency.
Both quantized conditions achieve the same $4\times$ KV cache compression (5.25~MB vs.\ 21~MB at bf16), so the latency difference between uniform and MCKP reflects only the mixed-precision dispatch overhead, not a memory trade-off.

\paragraph{Caveat.}
These measurements use PyTorch-level per-head \texttt{quantize}/\texttt{dequantize} calls and do \emph{not} represent optimized CUDA kernel performance.
Production inference engines such as vLLM and TensorRT-LLM employ fused attention kernels that batch dequantization with the matrix multiply, amortizing the per-head dispatch cost that dominates the gap between uniform and MCKP in our simulation.
The 8.5\% overhead reported here should therefore be treated as an upper bound on the mixed-precision penalty; real-world deployment with fused kernels would likely narrow or eliminate this gap.
The limitation noted in Section~\ref{sec:conclusion}---that we report no real-kernel latency benchmarks---remains: the numbers above quantify simulation overhead, not production inference speedup.

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Problem Formulation}
\label{sec:formulation}

We formulate per-head KV cache quantization as a constrained optimization problem.
Let $h \in \{1,\ldots,H\}$ index the KV heads across all layers, $b_h \in \mathcal{B}$ the bit-width assigned to head $h$, and $\mathcal{B} = \{2, 4, 8, 16\}$ the set of hardware-aligned bit-widths.
Each head $h$ has a profiled distortion function $d_h(b)$, the PPL increase when head $h$ is quantized to $b$ bits.
Given a memory budget ratio $R$ (e.g., $R = 0.25$ for $4\times$ compression), we seek the allocation that minimizes total distortion:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:objective}
\min_{\{b_h\}} \sum_{h=1}^{H} d_h(b_h) \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \frac{1}{H \cdot 16}\sum_{h=1}^{H} b_h \leq R.
\end{equation}

This is a multiple-choice knapsack problem (MCKP): each head selects one bit-width from $\mathcal{B}$ to minimize total distortion under a budget.
RateQuant~\cite{zuo2026ratequant} solves this for consecutive bit-widths $\{2,3,4,5,6\}$, where the polymatroid structure of the feasible set guarantees that greedy allocation is optimal (their Proposition 6).
Hardware-aligned bit-widths $\{2,4,8,16\}$ are non-consecutive; the gaps break this structure, and greedy is no longer guaranteed to match the MCKP optimum.
Moreover, as we show in \S\ref{sec:compounding}, the objective itself is not well-behaved: distortions from 2-bit heads compound non-additively, making unconstrained optimization unreliable.

\subsection{PPL-based Per-Head Profiling}
\label{sec:profiling}

We use symmetric per-channel quantization with per-head granularity: each KV head is independently quantized to one of the candidate bit-widths $\mathcal{B} = \{2, 4, 8, 16\}$, without per-token scaling.
We profile each head by quantizing it to each $b \in \mathcal{B}$ while keeping all other heads at bf16, and measuring the resulting PPL increase $d_h(b)$ on a calibration set of 64 sequences $\times$ 2048 tokens from WikiText-2 train.
This yields a distortion table of $H \times |\mathcal{B}|$ entries that serves as input to the solver.

The optimization in Eq.~\eqref{eq:objective} relies on an additive distortion assumption: the total PPL degradation from a mixed-precision allocation is approximately $\sum_h d_h(b_h)$.
This holds when per-head distortions are small and independent---a reasonable approximation for 4-bit and 8-bit quantization, where individual head distortions are typically below 0.1 PPL.
At 2-bit, however, individual distortions are orders of magnitude larger, and the additive assumption breaks down.

\subsection{Compounding Effect}
\label{sec:compounding}

When many heads are simultaneously quantized to 2-bit, per-head distortions compound non-additively: truncation errors from multiple 2-bit heads accumulate through the residual stream, and once their count exceeds the network's capacity to compensate, PPL degrades super-linearly.

Unconstrained solvers are blind to this effect because the additive objective underestimates the cost of large-scale 2-bit assignment.
On Qwen2.5-7B, unconstrained greedy assigns 68 heads to 2-bit and achieves 32.45 PPL; unconstrained MCKP assigns 75 heads and achieves 36.92---both far worse than uniform 4-bit (15.45).
Standard-resolution profiling detects non-zero $d_h(2)$ for only 37 of 112 heads; the remaining 75 heads have $d_h(2) \approx 0$, so most 2-bit assignments appear free to the additive objective.
The resulting additive prediction is $\sum_h d_h(b_h) + \text{baseline} \approx 11$ PPL---$3\text{--}4\times$ lower than the actual 32--37 PPL, because the compounding interaction between simultaneously quantized heads is invisible to per-head profiling.

This compounding is specific to 2-bit quantization and is \emph{super-additive}: quantizing the top-$k$ most sensitive heads to 2-bit simultaneously produces PPL $1.56\times$ the sum of their individual distortions at $k{=}8$, growing to $4.89\times$ at $k{=}64$.
A random-$k$ control confirms that sensitivity ordering matters: at $k{=}64$, random selection yields 55.94 PPL vs.\ 22.95 for top-$k$, because random draws are more likely to include high-distortion heads that amplify compounding.
At 4-bit and above, per-head distortions are small enough that additivity holds even when all heads share the same bit-width (uniform 4-bit achieves 15.45 PPL, close to the sum-of-distortions prediction).
The qualitative difference arises because 2-bit quantization has far larger per-head distortion, saturating the error tolerance of the residual stream.

The relationship between $k$ and PPL is U-shaped (Figure~\ref{fig:compounding}, Appendix~\ref{sec:compounding_appendix}): at $k{=}0$ (uniform 4-bit), PPL is 15.45; allowing a few 2-bit heads reduces PPL by freeing budget for 8-bit heads on sensitive positions, reaching a minimum of 11.23 at $k{=}8$; beyond $k{=}8$, compounding drives PPL upward.

\subsection{Cardinality Constraint}
\label{sec:cardinality}

The compounding effect motivates a simple addition to Eq.~\eqref{eq:objective}: a cardinality constraint on 2-bit assignments.
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:constraint}
\sum_{h=1}^{H} \mathbb{1}[b_h = 2] \leq k.
\end{equation}

The constraint parameter $k$ is selected by a small-scale sweep on calibration data: solve the constrained problem for each candidate $k$, evaluate calibration PPL, and pick the value at the minimum of the U-shaped curve (Figure~\ref{fig:compounding}).
For Qwen2.5-7B with 112 KV heads, $k{=}8$ ($\sim$7\% of heads).

The constraint is a single linear inequality, compatible with any solver---greedy, MCKP, or integer linear programming---and adds negligible overhead to the optimization.

One might expect Layer-0 heads to require explicit protection.
On Qwen2.5-7B, however, the profiler assigns very high 2-bit distortion to Layer-0 heads (L0H0: $+$31.9\%, L0H2: $+$23.2\%, L0H3: $+$11.7\% relative to bf16), so no solver would assign them 2-bit under any reasonable budget.
Running the solver with and without manual Layer-0 exclusion produces identical allocations; no manual rule is needed.

\paragraph{Restoring additivity.}
The additive objective treats per-head distortions as independent, but 2-bit compounding violates this once many 2-bit heads coexist.
Restricting 2-bit heads to $k \ll H$ pushes the allocation back into a near-additive regime where the profiler's prediction agrees with measured PPL.
For Qwen2.5-7B, $k{=}8$ ($\sim$7\% of heads) is few enough that compounding contributes negligibly, consistent with the U-shaped curve minimum (Figure~\ref{fig:compounding}).
This explains the constrained--unconstrained gap in Table~\ref{tab:solver_equiv}: constrained greedy operates inside the additive regime (11.23 PPL, matching the ${\approx}11$ additive estimate), while unconstrained solvers extrapolate outside it and miss true PPL by $3\text{--}4\times$.

\subsection{Solver Equivalence}
\label{sec:solver_equiv}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Solver comparison on Qwen2.5-7B at $4\times$ compression. Unconstrained solvers over-allocate 2-bit heads and fail catastrophically. The cardinality constraint ($k{\leq}8$) makes greedy and MCKP produce identical allocations.\protect\footnotemark}
\label{tab:solver_equiv}
\begin{tabular}{llcccc}
\toprule
Solver & Constraint & PPL & \multicolumn{3}{c}{Bit distribution} \\
 & & & 2-bit & 4-bit & 8/16-bit \\
\midrule
Greedy & None & 32.45 & 68 & 30 & 14 \\
MCKP & None & 36.92 & 75 & 33 & 4 \\
Greedy & $k{\leq}8$ & 11.23 & 8 & 100 & 4 \\
MCKP & $k{\leq}8$ & 11.23 & 8 & 100 & 4 \\
Uniform & --- & 15.45 & 0 & 112 & 0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}
\footnotetext{Standard-resolution profiling for illustration; main results (Table~\ref{tab:main_results}) use high-resolution profiling (10.91 PPL).}

With the cardinality constraint, greedy and MCKP produce identical results (Table~\ref{tab:solver_equiv}): same PPL (11.23), same bit distribution on Qwen2.5-7B.

\paragraph{Empirical observation.}
When $k \ll H$ and the top-$k$ heads ranked by marginal benefit $d_h(4) - d_h(2)$ contain no ties, constrained greedy and MCKP produce identical per-head allocations.
The constraint restricts the feasible set so that the only decision is which $k$ heads to assign 2-bit; the marginal-benefit ordering determines this uniquely, so greedy's local choice coincides with the global optimum.
We verify this across three architectures: Qwen2.5-7B (112/112 heads identical), Llama-3.1-8B (256/256), Mistral-7B-v0.3 (255/256; the single difference is a zero-distortion tie with no PPL impact).
Without the constraint, greedy cascades into excessive 2-bit allocation, as each additional 2-bit head looks locally beneficial but contributes to compounding.
Practitioners can therefore use the simpler $O(H \log H)$ greedy algorithm without sacrificing optimality.

A per-layer brute-force oracle on Qwen2.5-7B confirms near-optimality: of 8 layers with non-trivial allocations, MCKP matches the oracle on 5; the remaining 3 have a maximum gap of 0.018 PPL (0.4\% of the total improvement), closing the optimality chain from greedy through MCKP to brute-force.

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

\paragraph{Models.}
We evaluate seven models spanning five architecture families and diverse attention configurations:
\textbf{Qwen2.5-7B} (GQA 7:1, 28 layers $\times$ 4 KV heads = 112),
\textbf{Qwen2.5-14B} (GQA 5:1, 48 $\times$ 8 = 384),
\textbf{Yi-1.5-9B} (GQA 8:1, 48 $\times$ 4 = 192),
\textbf{Llama-3.1-8B} (GQA 4:1, 32 $\times$ 8 = 256),
\textbf{Mistral-7B-v0.3} (GQA 4:1, 32 $\times$ 8 = 256, sliding window attention),
\textbf{Gemma-2-9B} (GQA 2:1, 42 $\times$ 8 = 336, \texttt{head\_dim}=256, attention logit softcapping),
and \textbf{Pythia-1.4B} (MHA 1:1, 24 $\times$ 16 = 384).
This selection spans GQA ratios from 1:1 (MHA) to 8:1 and covers standard, sliding-window, softcapped, and multi-head attention.

\paragraph{Evaluation.}
We report perplexity (PPL, $\downarrow$) on the WikiText-2 test split (298{,}938 tokens) under bf16 inference with stride 512.
For downstream evaluation, we use ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag, PIQA, and WinoGrande under 0-shot prompting.

\paragraph{Profiling.}
Per-head sensitivity is measured by independently quantizing each head to each candidate bit-width in $\{2, 4, 8, 16\}$ and recording the PPL increase over the bf16 baseline.
All results use high-resolution profiling (64 sequences $\times$ 2048 tokens from WikiText-2 train); Appendix~\ref{sec:profiling_resolution} compares against standard-resolution profiling (16 seq $\times$ 512 tok).

\paragraph{Compression and constraint.}
The default target is 4$\times$ compression (average 4 bits, budget ratio 0.25).
The cardinality constraint limits 2-bit assignments to at most $k$ heads, where $k$ is selected via the compounding sweep described in \S\ref{sec:method} ($k{=}8$ for Qwen-7B, $k{=}2$ for Yi-1.5 and Llama, $k{=}4$ for Mistral, $k{=}8$ for Gemma-2; Qwen-14B and Pythia $k$ values pending).

\paragraph{Baselines.}
We compare against:
(1)~uniform 4-bit quantization;
(2)~unconstrained greedy allocation (RateQuant-style~\cite{zuo2026ratequant});
(3)~unconstrained MCKP allocation;
(4)~an L0-random heuristic that assigns Layer-0 heads to 8-bit and $k$ random non-Layer-0 heads to 2-bit, with remaining heads at 4-bit (5 seeds; we report mean$\pm$std and median);
and (5)~a naive positional heuristic that assigns Layer-0 to 8-bit and the $k$ deepest heads to 2-bit.

